heap and free store are same name?

Yes — in **C/C++ terminology, “heap” and “free store” are often used almost interchangeably**, 
but technically they are not exactly the same concept.

### C++

* **Free store** → memory obtained using `new` / `delete`

  int* p = new int(10);
  delete p;

* **Heap** → commonly refers to dynamically allocated memory, especially memory managed by the runtime/allocator.

### Simple view

| Term           | Commonly associated with          |
| -------------- | --------------------------------- |
| **Free store** | C++ `new` / `delete`              |
| **Heap**       | Dynamic memory / memory allocator |
| **Stack**      | Automatic/local variables         |

So when learning C++, it's useful to remember:

> **`new/delete` → free store**
> **`malloc/free` → heap**

But don't take that as a strict physical-memory distinction. 
On most implementations, the underlying memory may come from the **same general dynamic-memory area/allocator**.

Also, **“free store” does NOT mean “free memory.”** 
It means the C++ memory area from which dynamic objects can be allocated.
